# Black Hand OS — Waveshare 1.83inch LCD Module kernel fragment
#
# Display: 240x284 ST7789P on SPI0, driven by DRM panel-mipi-dbi
# Touch:   NONE. The module's CST816D is not wired up.
#
# ── Framebuffers we must actively turn OFF ─────────────────────────────────
#
# IMPORTANT: a Buildroot kernel fragment is MERGED onto bcm2711_defconfig.
# Deleting a line from this file does NOT unset the option — the base
# defconfig's value survives. bcm2711_defconfig carries CONFIG_FB_BCM2708=y
# and CONFIG_FB_SIMPLE=y, so both need an explicit "is not set" here.
#
# Why they must go: each registers its own framebuffer. The VideoCore's
# firmware fb claims fb0 even with nothing attached, fbcon binds to fb0
# FIRST, and the SPI panel ends up as fb1 with no console on it — a black
# screen that looks exactly like a driver failure. With both disabled the
# panel is the only framebuffer in the system and fbcon takes it over
# automatically the moment the driver registers.
# CONFIG_FB_BCM2708 is not set
# CONFIG_FB_SIMPLE is not set

# Base defconfig has these as =m for the HyperPixel's GT911 touch. Harmless
# (nothing loads them) but turned off so the config states the intent.
# CONFIG_TOUCHSCREEN_GOODIX is not set
# CONFIG_I2C_GPIO is not set

# ── SPI ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CONFIG_SPI=y
CONFIG_SPI_MASTER=y
CONFIG_SPI_BCM2835=y

# ── DRM tiny + MIPI DBI panel ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# EVERYTHING HERE IS BUILT IN (=y), DELIBERATELY.
#
# This started as =m so panel.bin could be re-tuned without a kernel rebuild.
# The module path then failed three separate ways on real hardware:
#
#   1. bcm2711_defconfig sets CONFIG_MODULE_COMPRESS_XZ=y. The host depmod
#      could not parse the resulting .ko.xz files, so panel-mipi-dbi was
#      silently OMITTED from modules.dep — modprobe reported it "not found"
#      even though the file was installed.
#   2. busybox insmod has no decompressor, so the .ko.xz could not be
#      loaded by hand either. Buildroot's BR2_PACKAGE_KMOD is the library
#      only; busybox is the sole modprobe/insmod on this target.
#   3. Loading by hand then failed with "Unknown symbol
#      drm_gem_dma_dumb_create" because insmod does not resolve the DRM
#      helper chain, and modules.dep — the thing that records it — was the
#      very file that was incomplete.
#
# The panel is this device's ONLY output. Putting it behind the most
# fragile subsystem in the image was the wrong call. This mirrors what
# this fragment already does for the SIM7600's USB-serial drivers, and for
# the same reason: "building them in (=y) removes the dependency on
# modprobe/udev entirely".
#
# The helpers are named explicitly rather than relying on
# DRM_PANEL_MIPI_DBI's selects: a select from a tristate can resolve to =m
# and reintroduce exactly the dependency chain this is meant to eliminate.
#
# DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION is what turns the DRM device into the /dev/fb that
# fbcon (and therefore notcurses) needs. Without it you get a working panel
# with no console on it.
CONFIG_DRM=y
CONFIG_DRM_KMS_HELPER=y
CONFIG_DRM_GEM_DMA_HELPER=y
CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION=y
CONFIG_DRM_MIPI_DBI=y
CONFIG_DRM_PANEL_MIPI_DBI=y

# ── Built-in panel init blob ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Built-in drivers probe before the ext4 rootfs is mounted, so a built-in
# panel-mipi-dbi calling request_firmware("panel.bin") would get -ENOENT —
# and request_firmware() never retries. The blob must therefore live inside
# the kernel image.
#
# EXTRA_FIRMWARE_DIR has no leading '/', so the kernel resolves it against
# $(srctree) (see drivers/base/firmware_loader/builtin/Makefile). The
# BLACKHAND_INSTALL_PANEL_FIRMWARE hook in external.mk copies panel.bin
# into $(LINUX_DIR)/firmware/ before the kernel builds. If that hook does
# not run, the kernel build fails loudly on a missing input rather than
# producing an image with a dark screen.
#
# Trade-off accepted: re-tuning panel.txt now costs a kernel rebuild.
CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE="panel.bin"
CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE_DIR="firmware"

# ── Backlight ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# gpio-backlight, matching dtparam=backlight-gpio=24 in config.txt.
# NOT pwm-backlight: the Pi's onboard headphone output uses the same PWM
# block, and rcS falls back to onboard audio when no USB card is present.
CONFIG_BACKLIGHT_CLASS_DEVICE=y
CONFIG_BACKLIGHT_GPIO=y

# ── Module compression: OFF ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# bcm2711_defconfig sets CONFIG_MODULE_COMPRESS_XZ=y, which installs modules
# as panel-mipi-dbi.ko.XZ. This system cannot load those:
#
#   - busybox insmod takes a raw .ko only; it has no decompressor.
#   - busybox modprobe derives a module's name by stripping ".ko" from the
#     modules.dep entry. "panel-mipi-dbi.ko.xz" does not end in ".ko", so the
#     match fails and it reports the module as "not found in modules.dep"
#     even though depmod indexed it correctly.
#
# That is exactly how this presented on hardware: the .ko.xz was installed,
# modules.dep was fine, and modprobe still could not see it — so nothing ever
# bound to spi0.0 and the panel stayed dark with no error anywhere.
#
# Buildroot does not install kmod's own modprobe by default (BR2_PACKAGE_KMOD
# is the library), so busybox is the only implementation present. Turning
# compression off is the fix. The modules cost a few hundred KB uncompressed
# on a 256 MB rootfs.
CONFIG_MODULE_COMPRESS_NONE=y
# CONFIG_MODULE_COMPRESS_XZ is not set
# CONFIG_MODULE_COMPRESS_GZIP is not set
# CONFIG_MODULE_COMPRESS_ZSTD is not set

# ── Firmware loader (for /lib/firmware/panel.bin) ──
CONFIG_FW_LOADER=y

# ── Framebuffer console ──
CONFIG_FB=y
CONFIG_FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE=y
CONFIG_VT=y
CONFIG_VT_CONSOLE=y

# ── devtmpfs (creates /dev/fb0, /dev/input/event* automatically) ──
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS=y
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS_MOUNT=y

# ── GPIO ──
CONFIG_GPIO_SYSFS=y
CONFIG_GPIOLIB=y

# ── I2C ──
# I2C_GPIO is gone with the HyperPixel: the bit-banged bus on pins 10/11
# existed only to reach the GT911 touch controller, and pins 10/11 are now
# SPI0 MOSI/SCLK. The hardware I2C controller stays available for future
# peripherals.
CONFIG_I2C=y
CONFIG_I2C_BCM2835=y
CONFIG_I2C_CHARDEV=y

# ── Input subsystem ──
CONFIG_INPUT=y
CONFIG_INPUT_EVDEV=y

# ── Touchscreen: NONE ──
# The 1.83inch module has a CST816D controller but it is deliberately not
# wired (TP_SDA/TP_SCL/TP_RST/TP_INT left unconnected). Input comes from the
# physical keypad / a USB keyboard, so the whole touch stack is dropped.

# ── USB HID (keyboard for console input and bring-up testing) ──
CONFIG_HID=y
CONFIG_HID_GENERIC=y
CONFIG_USB_HID=y
CONFIG_INPUT_KEYBOARD=y

# ── VFAT support (mount boot partition) ──
CONFIG_VFAT_FS=y
CONFIG_FAT_FS=y
CONFIG_NLS_CODEPAGE_437=y
CONFIG_NLS_ISO8859_1=y

# ── Audio ──
#
# Three layers are needed for ALSA to work end-to-end:
#
#   1. Core ALSA subsystem (SND, SND_PCM, SND_TIMER)
#      SND       — the main ALSA kernel framework
#      SND_PCM   — PCM device support; snd_pcm_open() needs this
#      SND_TIMER — high-resolution timer ALSA uses internally for
#                  scheduling audio callbacks; without it PCM devices
#                  may fail to open or stutter
#
#   2. Mixer support
#      SND_MIXER_OSS — exposes /dev/mixer so snd_mixer_open() works
#
#   3. Hardware drivers
#      SND_BCM2835   — Pi 4 onboard audio (3.5mm jack + HDMI audio)
#      SND_USB_AUDIO — USB audio adapters; Pi 4's onboard DAC is low
#                      quality, USB adapters are common in practice
#
CONFIG_SOUND=y
CONFIG_SND=y
CONFIG_SND_PCM=y
CONFIG_SND_TIMER=y
CONFIG_SND_MIXER_OSS=y
CONFIG_SND_BCM2835=y
CONFIG_SND_USB_AUDIO=y

# ── Bluetooth ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Core BT subsystem + profiles:
#   BREDR  — Classic Bluetooth (A2DP, HFP, pairing)
#   RFCOMM — Serial over BT; HFP uses this for AT command signalling
#   SCO    — Synchronous audio channel; required for HFP mic/earpiece
#   LE     — Bluetooth Low Energy
#   HIDP   — BT HID (keyboards, mice, gamepads)
#   BNEP   — BT PAN (network over Bluetooth)
#
CONFIG_BT=y
CONFIG_BT_BREDR=y
CONFIG_BT_RFCOMM=y
CONFIG_BT_SCO=y
CONFIG_BT_HS=y
CONFIG_BT_LE=y
CONFIG_BT_HIDP=y
CONFIG_BT_BNEP=y

# ── Serial device bus (serdev) — REQUIRED for krnbt=on ──
# dtparam=krnbt=on in config.txt tells the RPi firmware to enable the
# kernel-managed BT attachment path. The kernel uses the serdev framework
# to bind the BCM43455 driver directly to /dev/ttyAMA0 (PL011 UART).
#
# Without these three options, serdev probe fails silently:
#   - hci0 never appears in /sys/class/bluetooth/
#   - bluetoothd starts but finds no adapter
#   - rfkill shows nothing to unblock
#
# SERIAL_DEV_BUS         — the serdev bus itself
# SERIAL_DEV_CTRL_TTYPORT— lets serdev bind to a standard ttyAMA0 port
# BT_HCIUART_SERDEV      — auto-selected when SERIAL_DEV_BUS + BT_HCIUART_BCM
#                          are both =y, but explicitly set here for clarity
CONFIG_SERIAL_DEV_BUS=y
CONFIG_SERIAL_DEV_CTRL_TTYPORT=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART_SERDEV=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART_BCM=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART_H4=y

# ── SIM7600 cellular modem (USB serial AT ports) ──
# bcm2711_defconfig builds these as modules (=m), but our custom init has no
# module autoloading, so /dev/ttyUSB* never appeared (confirmed on hardware:
# lsusb saw 1e0e:9001 SimTech, zero ttyUSB ports, "module option not found").
# Building them in (=y) removes the dependency on modprobe/udev entirely.
#
# USB_SERIAL         — the USB serial core
# USB_SERIAL_WWAN    — shared layer for cellular modems (selected by OPTION,
#                      stated explicitly for clarity)
# USB_SERIAL_OPTION  — the driver that matches SimTech 1e0e:9001 and exposes
#                      the modem's interfaces as /dev/ttyUSB0..4 (AT port is
#                      usually USB2, sometimes USB3)
CONFIG_USB_SERIAL=y
CONFIG_USB_SERIAL_WWAN=y
CONFIG_USB_SERIAL_OPTION=y

# rfkill — lets you block/unblock the radio at runtime.
# "rfkill unblock bluetooth" in rcS brings the chip out of soft-block.
CONFIG_RFKILL=y
CONFIG_RFKILL_INPUT=y

# ── Crypto — ALL required for Bluetooth security ────────────────────────
#
# CMAC + AES  — Classic BT: bluetoothd calls crypto_alloc_shash("cmac(aes)")
#               at startup. If either is missing, hci0 gets
#               "Unable to create CMAC crypto context" and bluetoothd exits.
#
# ECDH        — BLE Secure Connections pairing (phase 2 key exchange).
#               Without it, BLE devices refuse to pair with error
#               "Authentication Failed" or "Pairing not supported".
#
# SHA256      — BLE key derivation (f4, f5, f6 functions in the spec).
#
# CCM         — BLE link-layer encryption (AES-CCM cipher mode).
#               Without it, encrypted BLE connections drop immediately.
#
CONFIG_CRYPTO_CMAC=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_AES=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_ECDH=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_SHA256=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_CCM=y

# In: blackhand-external/board/blackhand/linux/bcm2711.config

# ── Framebuffer console font ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The UI needs a 40 x 15 character grid (PHONE_COLS / PHONE_SCREEN_ROWS in
# blackhand-ui's config.h). On a 240x284 panel that constrains the font to
# 6 px wide — 240 / 6 = 40 columns exactly.
#
#   ProFont6x11  6x11  ->  40 cols x 25 rows   <-- selected
#   6x10         6x10  ->  40 cols x 28 rows   (smaller glyphs, more rows)
#   VGA8x16      8x16  ->  30 cols x 17 rows   (bigger, but 10 cols short)
#   TER16x32    16x32  ->  15 cols x  8 rows   (the old HyperPixel font)
#
# ProFont6x11 is the largest built-in font that still delivers 40 columns,
# and 25 rows leaves the 15-row phone plane centred with margin.
#
# NOTE: TER16x32 on the 480-wide HyperPixel gave only 30 columns, so the
# rightmost 10 columns of every screen were being silently clipped by the
# clamp in create_phone_plane(). This panel is the first time the 40-column
# layout renders in full.
#
# VGA8x16 is kept compiled in as a fallback: if 6x11 proves unreadable at
# this pixel density, switching is a one-word change in cmdline.txt (and a
# 30-column UI relayout).
CONFIG_FONTS=y
CONFIG_FONT_6x11=y
CONFIG_FONT_8x16=y

# NOTE: CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE *is* now used — for the panel's panel.bin, see
# the "Built-in panel init blob" section above. The warning below is about
# the BLUETOOTH firmware specifically, and it still stands. The difference is
# the DIRECTORY: the panel uses a relative EXTRA_FIRMWARE_DIR that the kernel
# resolves against $(srctree), with a Buildroot hook putting the file there.
# The absolute "/lib/firmware" below is what does not work.
#
# The BT firmware is intentionally NOT embedded this way.
# CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE_DIR="/lib/firmware" resolves on the BUILD HOST (macOS),
# which does not have /lib/firmware, so nothing gets embedded and the kernel
# falls back to requesting the file at runtime — but by then it may not be
# mounted yet. Instead, the firmware lives in the rootfs overlay at
# board/blackhand/overlay/lib/firmware/brcm/ and is loaded by the serdev
# driver after the ext4 rootfs is mounted (which happens before serdev probes
# the BCM chip when krnbt=on is absent from config.txt).